  {
   "id": "-2p6pABw3-zZHt5CWNPrB",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 1937.3213772243932,
   "y": 4530.861111111111,
   "width": 790.5794677734375,
   "height": 425,
   "angle": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#e03131",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 1,
   "opacity": 100,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "index": "bOG",
   "roundness": null,
   "seed": 497908583,
   "version": 241,
   "versionNonce": 14575997,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1791898805609,
   "created": 1791881451747,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "The agent makes one decision per task: anchor, a neighbour, or the cloud. All\nsteps then run on that node.\nThe node runs the steps in order. A small executor on the node handles\ndependencies. Parallel branches can share that node’s CPU and GPU. The agent\ndoesn’t schedule steps. That’s the node’s local job, and it’s trivial.\nWhat the DAG still gives you: the agent’s state can carry task features, so the\nagent sees that tasks differ:\n- total work\n- critical-path work (the longest chain)\n- input size\n- deadline\n\nLatency = send the input to the node + wait in its queue + run the critical path\non that node + send the result. There is no transfer between steps, because all\nsteps are on one node.\nFailure: the whole task is retried from the input the anchor stored. There’s no\npartial state spread over several zones.",
   "fontSize": 20,
   "baseFontSize": null,
   "fontFamily": 5,
   "textAlign": "center",
   "verticalAlign": "middle",
   "containerId": "Js8Qhieu5x-X9a_YKDfIR",
   "originalText": "The agent makes one decision per task: anchor, a neighbour, or the cloud. All steps then run on that node.\nThe node runs the steps in order. A small executor on the node handles dependencies. Parallel branches can share that node’s CPU and GPU. The agent doesn’t schedule steps. That’s the node’s local job, and it’s trivial.\nWhat the DAG still gives you: the agent’s state can carry task features, so the agent sees that tasks differ:\n- total work\n- critical-path work (the longest chain)\n- input size\n- deadline\n\nLatency = send the input to the node + wait in its queue + run the critical path on that node + send the result. There is no transfer between steps, because all steps are on one node.\nFailure: the whole task is retried from the input the anchor stored. There’s no partial state spread over several zones.",
   "autoResize": true,
   "lineHeight": 1.25,
   "labelPosition": null
  },
